{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "the social network is a gripping drama that chronicles the founding of facebook and the subsequent legal battles surrounding it. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the film moves at a breakneck pace, driven by sharp dialogue and a haunting score by trent reznor and atticus ross. jesse eisenberg delivers a standout performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him not as a villain but as a socially awkward genius driven by a need for validation and status. the narrative unfolds through dual timelines, showing the creation of the website alongside the depositions where former friends and business partners sue zuckerberg. andrew garfield shines as eduardo saverin, the co-founder whose betrayal forms the emotional core of the story. the cinematography is cool and detached, mirroring the isolating nature of the digital world the characters inhabit. unlike many biopics, this movie avoids glorifying its subject, instead presenting a complex portrait of ambition, envy, and loneliness. it captures the zeitgeist of a generation connecting online while drifting apart in real life. the ending, with zuckerberg refreshing a profile page, leaves a lingering sense of emptiness that defines the entire film. it is a modern tragedy disguised as a tech success story, masterfully executed in every frame.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.6078441}, {"token": " social", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " network", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.29455283}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " gripping", "logprob": -0.28906244}, {"token": " drama", "logprob": -0.02494482}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.009584013}, {"token": " chron", "logprob": -0.0002769019}, {"token": "icles", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " founding", "logprob": -0.0008376768}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " faceb